<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<RunSettings>
    <RunConfiguration>
        <EnvironmentVariables>
            <!--
              glibc's allocator held to two arenas in the test host. glibc gives a thread an arena of
              its own, every app the suite makes starts threads of its own in the GPU's driver, and
              each app left its arenas holding about ten megabytes it had freed, which MemoryGuard
              counts as held. A suite of a thousand apps then reached its cap holding a third of it.
              An app that opens a sound device leaves almost none behind, for a reason not traced,
              so the suite stayed under while each app opened one, and stopped short once a run with
              no window opened none. Read as the process starts, so it is set here rather than from
              the suite, whose runtime has made its arenas by then. Other C libraries ignore it.
            -->
            <MALLOC_ARENA_MAX>2</MALLOC_ARENA_MAX>
        </EnvironmentVariables>
    </RunConfiguration>
</RunSettings>
